\chapter{Stress Tests}
\label{ch:stress}

The tests of the other chapters check that a function does what it should, once, on a quiet machine. A kernel that has to run for days fails in
another way: two processes, a timer interrupt and a second CPU meet in a place that nobody had thought of, and once in a million times the
machine freezes. This chapter is a program that makes the million come sooner. \texttt{stress} starts many processes and threads that
all use the kernel at the same time, each from another side, and checks every result it can check.

\paragraph{The modes.} \texttt{/musl/stress MODE SECONDS SEED MB [HOST TCPPORT UDPPORT]} runs one mode for the given time. The modes are
\texttt{fork} (fork, exec, exit and wait in bulk, children killed while they sleep), \texttt{pipe} (pipes with a writer that is killed or a reader that
leaves), \texttt{futex} (mutexes, condition variables, \texttt{FUTEX\_WAIT} with time-outs, wake and requeue, threads that start and end), \texttt{signal}
(signals sent to a process that runs, sleeps, waits in \texttt{read} or in a futex), \texttt{mmap} (anonymous memory beyond the size of the RAM, so that the
kernel swaps, with copy-on-write after \texttt{fork}), \texttt{fs} (files created, written, read back, renamed, truncated and removed; the argument
\texttt{MB} is not used, the directory is given as \texttt{fs:DIR}), \texttt{sock} (socket pairs, and TCP and UDP echo servers of the host
when the three last arguments are given), \texttt{timer} (sleeps, time-outs of \texttt{ppoll} and of the futex: never too early) and \texttt{all}, which starts every mode
as a process of its own, the file system mode three times (on \texttt{/tmp} with a Minix~v3 disk, on \texttt{/mnt} with a Minix~v2 disk, and on the root disk).

\paragraph{What counts as a failure.} A check that fails prints \texttt{user FAIL:}. A mode that hangs prints no more progress lines: every mode
prints \texttt{stress: MODE n} every few seconds, and the host program \texttt{tools/stress\_test.py} (\texttt{make stress-test}) watches
them, asks the machine through the monitor of qemu (registers of every CPU) when something stops, and looks for exceptions and panics in the output.

